-- Poh Yuan Nie v Public Prosecutor [2022] SGCA 74
-- Singapore Penal Code (Cap 224, 2008 Rev Ed) s 415 — Cheating
--
-- A worked example: the Court of Appeal's reasoning, executed.
--
-- This file is the human-auditable MODEL — the statute as typed code. It encodes
-- the *2008 version* of s 415 (the one before the court: two limbs + Explanations
-- 1-3 only — NOT the current 5-Explanation text in cheating-415.l4) and runs the
-- court's reasoning on hand-picked scenarios:
--
--   1. a CASE SPLIT over the four candidate readings of "dishonest"   (GD [18])
--   2. a COUNTEREXAMPLE that refutes the applicants' reading          (GD [28])
--   3. a single-scenario DON'T-CARE check illustrating that their
--      reading renders Explanation 1 "otiose" — surplusage             (GD [30], [32])
--
-- The DETERMINISTIC, EXHAUSTIVE versions of (2) and (3) — proved over the whole
-- fact space rather than checked at a point — belong in a decision procedure, not
-- in brute-force enumeration here. See the companion Z3 proof
-- `cheating-415-surplusage.z3.py`, which PROVES the surplusage theorem (unsat of
-- its negation) and lets the solver FIND the counterexample class itself. L4 is
-- the model; the solver is the engine it lowers to.


-- ===========================================================================
-- The statute, verbatim (2008 version, reproduced at GD [5])
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- s 24. Whoever does anything with the intention of causing wrongful gain to
--       one person, or wrongful loss to another person, is said to do that
--       thing DISHONESTLY.                      <- "the s 24 requirement": property.
--
-- s 415. Whoever, by deceiving any person, whether or not such deception was
--        the sole or main inducement, fraudulently or dishonestly induces the
--        person so deceived to deliver any property to any person, or to consent
--        that any person shall retain any property, or intentionally induces the
--        person so deceived to do or omit to do anything which he would not do
--        or omit to do if he were not so deceived, and which act or omission
--        causes or is likely to cause damage or harm to any person in body,
--        mind, reputation or property, is said to "cheat".
--
--   Explanation 1.  A dishonest concealment of facts is a deception within the
--                   meaning of this section.
--
-- Two alternative limbs (GD [6], following Knight Glenn Jeyasingam):
--   First  limb: by deceiving + (fraudulently OR s24-dishonestly) + induces
--                delivery/retention of PROPERTY.
--   Second limb: by deceiving + INTENTIONALLY induces an act/omission causing
--                harm in body, mind, reputation OR property.  (No s 24 here.)


-- ===========================================================================
-- The operative atoms of one cheating scenario
-- ===========================================================================

DECLARE `Cheating Scenario` HAS
    `by deceiving any person`                            IS A BOOLEAN  -- a positive deception
    `dishonest concealment of facts`                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- the Explanation 1 route
    `intent to cause wrongful property gain or loss`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- THE s 24 requirement
    `fraudulently`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `induces delivery or retention of property`          IS A BOOLEAN  -- first-limb actus reus
    `intentionally induces act or omission`              IS A BOOLEAN  -- second-limb mens rea
    `act or omission causes or is likely to cause harm`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `harm to body`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `harm to mind`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `harm to reputation`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `harm to property`                                   IS A BOOLEAN


-- ===========================================================================
-- The four candidate readings of "dishonest" in Explanation 1  (GD [18])
-- ===========================================================================

DECLARE Interpretation IS ONE OF
    `First Interpretation`    -- amicus: s24-concealment is a deception, but other concealments suffice too
    `Second Interpretation`   -- applicants: ONLY an s24-satisfying concealment is a deception
    `Third Interpretation`    -- PP / court below: "dishonest" = ordinary character of the act
    `Fourth Interpretation`   -- the court: "dishonest" = intention to deceive; no extra mens rea


-- ===========================================================================
-- The hinge: when does a "dishonest concealment of facts" count as a deception?
-- ===========================================================================
-- The whole appeal turns on this one predicate. On every reading EXCEPT the
-- applicants', the concealment is a deception in the ordinary sense. On the
-- applicants' (Second) reading, "dishonest" imports the s 24 property gate.

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
      i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`dishonest concealment counts as a deception` s i MEANS
    CONSIDER i
    WHEN `Second Interpretation` THEN
            s's `dishonest concealment of facts`
        AND `the s 24 requirement is met` s
    OTHERWISE
        s's `dishonest concealment of facts`

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 24 requirement is met` s IF
    s's `intent to cause wrongful property gain or loss`


-- ===========================================================================
-- s 415, isomorphic (inert style): verbatim text inline, operative atoms lifted
-- ===========================================================================

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
      i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is a deception` s i IF
        s's `by deceiving any person`
    OR  `dishonest concealment counts as a deception` s i      -- Explanation 1

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
      i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `first limb` s i IF
         "Whoever, by deceiving any person,"
    ...  `there is a deception` s i
    ...  "whether or not such deception was the sole or main inducement,"
    ...  "fraudulently or dishonestly"
    ...  (   s's `fraudulently`
          OR `the s 24 requirement is met` s )
    ...  "induces the person so deceived to deliver any property to any person,"
    ...  "or to consent that any person shall retain any property,"
    ...  s's `induces delivery or retention of property`

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
      i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `second limb` s i IF
         `there is a deception` s i
    ...  "intentionally induces the person so deceived"
    ...  s's `intentionally induces act or omission`
    ...  "to do or omit to do anything which he would not do or omit to do if he were not so deceived,"
    ...  "and which act or omission causes or is likely to cause"
    ...  s's `act or omission causes or is likely to cause harm`
    ...  "damage or harm to any person in"
    ...  (   "body"
          .. s's `harm to body`
          .. "mind"
          .. s's `harm to mind`
          .. "reputation"
          .. s's `harm to reputation`
          .. "or property"
          .. s's `harm to property` )

GIVEN s IS A `Cheating Scenario`
      i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is said to cheat` s i IF
        `first limb` s i
    OR  `second limb` s i


-- ===========================================================================
-- GD [28]: the counterexample that refutes the applicants' reading
-- ===========================================================================
-- "Consider ... a candidate who submits a forged university degree to support
--  his successful application for an unpaid internship. There is clearly no
--  transfer of property involved whatsoever ... However, any reasonable
--  layperson would agree that that candidate had dishonestly concealed the fact
--  that he did not graduate ... and had thereby cheated ... the employer."

`forged degree for unpaid internship` MEANS `Cheating Scenario` WITH
    `by deceiving any person`                            IS FALSE  -- isolate the concealment route
    `dishonest concealment of facts`                     IS TRUE   -- concealed non-graduation
    `intent to cause wrongful property gain or loss`     IS FALSE  -- "nothing but experience"
    `fraudulently`                                       IS FALSE
    `induces delivery or retention of property`          IS FALSE  -- no property changes hands
    `intentionally induces act or omission`              IS TRUE   -- induced the employer to take him on
    `act or omission causes or is likely to cause harm`  IS TRUE
    `harm to body`                                       IS FALSE
    `harm to mind`                                       IS FALSE
    `harm to reputation`                                 IS TRUE   -- deceived the employer
    `harm to property`                                   IS FALSE

-- The case split, run over the forged-degree facts.
-- Every reading convicts EXCEPT the applicants' — which acquits the fraudster.
-- That lone FALSE is the "absurd state of affairs" of GD [28].
#EVAL `is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `First Interpretation`   -- TRUE
#EVAL `is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `Second Interpretation`  -- FALSE  <- the absurdity
#EVAL `is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `Third Interpretation`   -- TRUE
#EVAL `is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `Fourth Interpretation`  -- TRUE

#ASSERT      `is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `Fourth Interpretation`
#ASSERT NOT (`is said to cheat` `forged degree for unpaid internship` `Second Interpretation`)


-- ===========================================================================
-- GD [30], [32]: surplusage as a don't-care check (illustrative, single point)
-- ===========================================================================
-- The applicants' reading would render Explanation 1 "otiose". In boolean terms:
-- on the no-property facts, the value of `dishonest concealment of facts` cannot
-- change the verdict — it is a DON'T-CARE variable, doing no work. We test it by
-- cofactor: hold the internship facts fixed and flip the concealment bit.
-- (The EXHAUSTIVE proof over the whole no-property region is in the Z3 companion.)

GIVEN i IS AN Interpretation
      c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`internship second limb, concealment set to` i c MEANS
    `second limb` `the internship with concealment varied` i
    WHERE
        `the internship with concealment varied` MEANS `Cheating Scenario` WITH
            `by deceiving any person`                            IS FALSE
            `dishonest concealment of facts`                     IS c       -- <- the varied bit
            `intent to cause wrongful property gain or loss`     IS FALSE
            `fraudulently`                                       IS FALSE
            `induces delivery or retention of property`          IS FALSE
            `intentionally induces act or omission`              IS TRUE
            `act or omission causes or is likely to cause harm`  IS TRUE
            `harm to body`                                       IS FALSE
            `harm to mind`                                       IS FALSE
            `harm to reputation`                                 IS TRUE
            `harm to property`                                   IS FALSE

GIVEN i IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Explanation 1 is otiose for the second limb without property` i MEANS
    (`internship second limb, concealment set to` i TRUE)
        EQUALS
    (`internship second limb, concealment set to` i FALSE)

-- applicants' reading: FALSE / FALSE -> equal -> concealment is a don't-care -> otiose
#EVAL `internship second limb, concealment set to` `Second Interpretation` TRUE    -- FALSE
#EVAL `internship second limb, concealment set to` `Second Interpretation` FALSE   -- FALSE
-- court's reading:     TRUE  / FALSE -> differ -> concealment is LIVE -> real work
#EVAL `internship second limb, concealment set to` `Fourth Interpretation` TRUE    -- TRUE
#EVAL `internship second limb, concealment set to` `Fourth Interpretation` FALSE   -- FALSE

--   under the applicants' (Second) reading, Explanation 1 is otiose (surplusage)
#ASSERT      `Explanation 1 is otiose for the second limb without property` `Second Interpretation`
--   under the court's (Fourth) reading, Explanation 1 is live (no surplusage)
#ASSERT NOT (`Explanation 1 is otiose for the second limb without property` `Fourth Interpretation`)
